Chapter: RocCondEmptyBranch
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 "inspect: conditional empty list branch". The whole Roc case is one line:

     { result = if True {[]} else {[1, 2]} match result { [] => 42, _ => 0 } }

 and Roc expects 42.

 Chosen because it puts an EMPTY LIST LITERAL in one arm of a branch whose
 other arm is concrete, which is the join of the two things this tree changed
 today: an empty literal has no element to answer from, and a branch has to
 agree on a type its arms may each know only half of.

 Two adaptations, both standing ones this batch does not invent.

 LIST PATTERNS. Codex has no list patterns in `when`, so `match result { []
 => 42, _ => 0 }` is `list-length`. Every existing port makes the same
 substitution; it is the shape of the test that matters, not the spelling of
 the read.

 THE DECIMAL. Roc's numeric literals default to a decimal type and its
 harness prints `42.0`; Codex Integer prints `42`.

Section: Functions

  cond-empty : Integer -> Integer
  cond-empty (n) =
   let result = if True then [] else [1, 2]
   in if list-length result == 0 then 42 else 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (cond-empty 0))
  end
